WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.670
Well, let's get going
then and welcome everybody

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:09.820
to the Data Science Hangout.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:12.100
And a special welcome
to anyone who's

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:14.660
joining for the
first time today.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.520
So if this is your first
time joining, just to let you

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:21.730
know how things work here.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:26.520
It's an all audience
led informal session

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.280
where we want to focus
all of the data science

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.180
leadership questions that are
most important to you all.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:36.720
And so each week I'm joined
by a different featured leader

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.420
you get to pick the brain of.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.620
And so there's three ways
that you can ask questions.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.650
You could jump in live,
you could put questions

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:50.340
in the chat, and we
also have a Slido, which

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.990
I'll share the link to shortly.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.080
But just a reminder
that the conversation

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.800
is recorded and shared
up to YouTube for anybody

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.730
who misses it or if you want
to go back and check out

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.739
the recording.

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:08.910
But we really do want to make
this a open and welcome space

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:10.530
where everybody can participate.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.830
And we really want to
hear from everyone.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.630
And I'll say we
especially want to hear

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.660
from people from
underrepresented groups in data

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.720
science as well.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.390
And I'm so excited to
be joined by my co-host

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.840
for today, Chase
Carpenter, Is the director

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.890
of strategy and analytics
at the Chicago Cubs.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.690
And Chase, I'd
love to kick it off

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.940
by maybe having you
introduce yourself

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.770
and a little bit about
the work that you do.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:39.270
Yeah.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:39.840
Hey, Rachel.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:40.860
Hey, everybody.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:46.020
First off, sincere thanks for
scheduling this and giving me

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.670
the opportunity to chat today.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.280
So as Rachel said, my
name is Chase Carpenter.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.060
I'm the director of strategy and
analytics for the Chicago Cubs.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.230
I've been with the organization
for just over five years.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:02.010
Everything I work on is
on the business side.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.260
And in a lot of
ways I support most

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.990
of our major functional areas.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:11.610
So it's ticketing, marketing,
corporate partnerships,

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.010
food and beverage,
retail hospitality,

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:16.080
as well as our event
operations group.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.130
We think of our--

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.660
I sit on our strategy team.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.430
And so we think of
ourselves as, in a lot

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.110
of ways, an internal
resource or sort

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.810
of internal consulting group
for the rest of the business.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:29.400
Awesome.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.130
Thanks, Chase.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.370
Well, thanks for joining us.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.080
And while we're
waiting for questions

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.870
to come in from everyone,
I love to kick it off

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.570
with asking you maybe
what's something

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:45.965
that you're most excited about
with regards to data science?

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:48.260
Yeah, so it's a
really good question.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.760
I spent some time
thinking about it.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:53.600
I knew that would be
sort of front and center.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.450
So I spend a lot
of my time trying

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.820
to think about how to drive
value out of data science,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:03.720
and more broadly speaking,
sort of data as an asset.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.520
And so I think even if you just
look at the my title, director

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.820
of strategy and
analytics, I spend a lot

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.640
of time trying to find
areas of opportunity

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:16.040
where we can drive business
value out of the data

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:19.940
that we have or that we
can reasonably go collect.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.220
I think there's a wide
range of problems out there.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.420
Some problems that
are interesting,

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:24.960
some problems drive
business value.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.470
And so where I try
and focus my time

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:30.750
is sort of the
marriage of the two.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:35.300
And I think more broadly for
data science as a profession,

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:39.980
we've made some awesome
progress better integrating

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.800
data and analytics into
the business operations,

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.570
certainly with them sports, but
across all industry verticals.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:51.800
And so that's-- part of that
is due to tools and process.

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:55.070
And as well as at
the academic level,

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:58.580
some significant progress
in getting the right types

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:01.520
of course material into the
hands of students as they

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.800
come up through both
undergrad and graduate school.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.100
And it's been an
awesome evolution

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.480
that we're actually seeing
this marriage of putting data

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.220
into practice so
that you can drive

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:16.430
business value for whatever
organization you're a part of.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:19.220
Awesome.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.850
And so for some of us who maybe
are thinking about Moneyball

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:26.990
as our first example of
how you use R in sports,

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.630
can you give us a
few examples of how

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.960
your team's using data
science on the business

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:34.767
side of the house?

00:04:34.767 --> 00:04:37.370
Yeah, so when I
joined the organization,

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:40.820
my first title was assistant
director of advanced analytics.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.860
And pretty much everyone thinks
that is Moneyball, right?

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.230
So it's like player
analytics, it's R&amp;amp;D,

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:51.290
it's the book or the
movie, if you've seen that.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:53.210
For better or worse,
I don't really

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:56.030
have any direct interaction with
our baseball operations team.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:59.060
We've got a fantastic
R&amp;amp;D group downstairs

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.180
that they do some
pretty awesome work.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:08.000
On the business side,
a baseball team,

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.600
the majority of our revenue
and sort of our key business

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.830
really relates to ticketing.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.600
We host 81 games a year.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.870
And so there's a ton
of effort and emphasis

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.300
in thinking about
how do we match

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.810
the right product
with customers,

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.005
how do we drive demand
for our business.

00:05:24.005 --> 00:05:25.880
And so a lot of the
problems that I worked on

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:31.010
are related to ticket
pricing, inventory management.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:35.030
And then sort of the extension
of that is putting forward

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:36.740
a great guest experience.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:39.710
So on a sold out
game, we'll have

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:42.710
38, 40,000 guests in our venue.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:47.180
And so there's a big operation
to put forward the best guest

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.360
experience that we can.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.277
And so there's a lot of
work behind the scenes

00:05:50.277 --> 00:05:53.960
to really understand what do
our customers want, how can we

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.960
deliver products, and deliver
a guest experience that

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:57.850
meets their needs.

00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:00.650
And so that's where I spend
a lot of my time trying

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:06.050
to improve or sort of evolve
what those products are

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:09.747
and what the game day
presentation looks like.

00:06:09.747 --> 00:06:10.330
That's cool.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.430
Can I ask you like,
what's an example

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:15.100
of a change to the
guest experience that

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.455
would come out of data science?

00:06:17.455 --> 00:06:22.420
Yeah, so I think a lot-- so
the example I'd give really was

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:26.740
a byproduct of COVID, which
has been incredibly disruptive,

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:30.070
personally, professionally,
sort of across the board.

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:36.100
We were not able in 2020 to host
any guests at Wrigley Field.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.660
We ended up being able to
play a shortened season.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:41.050
But we took that downtime
as an organization

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:43.720
to ask some tough
questions on what

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:47.375
do we want the business to look
like in three to five years

00:06:47.375 --> 00:06:49.750
because we kind of had this
extra window of time in which

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:52.480
we could plan and prepare.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.590
And so from a game day
presentation and experience

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:58.990
standpoint, what
that turned into,

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:02.870
we completely overhauled
the access control process.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:06.398
So historically, we'd
have these long lines

00:07:06.398 --> 00:07:08.440
and you'd have to go
through this metal detector,

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:09.310
and it was just--

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:11.260
you'd have someone
poking in your bag.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:14.980
It was not a very
welcoming experience.

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:18.880
And so through some new
technology and changes

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.560
that we deployed in
our security footprint,

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:29.540
we're able to completely
overhaul that entire process.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:33.430
And once you get in the
venue, we put forward--

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:37.300
we moved cash from our
venue and put a big emphasis

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:41.380
on contactless ordering
for food and beverage.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:46.180
Data science specifically played
into that in several components

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:50.380
because it's thinking about
balancing the guest experience.

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:52.750
There are certainly some
financial components

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:54.940
to some trade-offs
that we had to evaluate

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:58.870
in terms of staffing and
what we could actually

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:02.500
deliver to our guests.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:08.380
But it is sort of data-- it
was a very data forward sort

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:11.062
of process, and understanding
the various stakeholders

00:08:11.062 --> 00:08:12.520
and what's important
to each group,

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:15.817
and how do we find
the right balance.

00:08:15.817 --> 00:08:16.560
Definitely.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:19.080
And Chris, I didn't see
your question in the chat

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:21.510
when I brought up
Moneyball before.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:25.330
Do you want to introduce
yourself and ask that question?

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:28.510
CHRIS: Oh, sure, why not?

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:30.670
So Chase, I'm a
data scientist here

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:32.799
at Air Force Special
Operations Command.

00:08:32.799 --> 00:08:35.230
And the question that
I had posted on there

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:37.900
is just, how much influence
did the movie Moneyball have

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:38.455
on your job?

00:08:42.249 --> 00:08:44.440
The short answer is a lot.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:45.130
I wish that--

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:51.920
Sorry, were you going to
say something else, Chris?

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:53.345
CHRIS: Oh, no, I'm good.

00:08:53.345 --> 00:08:54.260
OK.

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:56.960
The short answer is a lot.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.840
I was fortunate enough to play
baseball through high school,

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.237
in college, and had big
aspirations to try and play

00:09:03.237 --> 00:09:04.820
Minor League or Major
League Baseball,

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:07.640
and then to be a coach
or a scout or something.

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:11.060
For a variety of reasons
that didn't work out,

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:14.997
so I went into the
professional workforce.

00:09:14.997 --> 00:09:16.580
Fast forward eight
or nine years and I

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:18.830
had the opportunity to
join the Cubs organization

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:19.725
on the business side.

00:09:19.725 --> 00:09:21.350
And it was sort of
the perfect marriage

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:24.260
of what I had spent
being incredibly

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:28.880
passionate about for the
first 22 years of my life

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:31.970
and then married that with
where I had spent eight or nine

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:35.300
years on the professional side.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:39.262
I am a huge, in my heart of
hearts, a huge baseball nerd

00:09:39.262 --> 00:09:41.220
and so I still follow
FanGraphs and sort of all

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.790
the Moneyball type stuff.

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:46.850
And my personal
time doesn't play

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:50.870
as direct a role in what I
get to think about when I'm

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:52.160
wearing my official Cubs hat.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:56.950
CHRIS: Wonderful, thank you.

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:58.150
Thanks, chase.

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:01.320
I see there's a few anonymous
questions coming in as well.

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:05.080
So I just want to head over
to some of those from Slido.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:06.730
And the first one
was, how much do

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:11.890
the players know about analytics
and how it affects them?

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:15.850
You know, so I'd say,
first off, some of this

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:17.740
is a little bit of
speculation and more what

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:20.210
I've heard from my colleagues.

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:25.510
But I think really it runs
the gamut of some players

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:28.840
don't necessarily have much
appetite or interest in really

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:35.185
understanding the Moneyball type
metrics that we can produce.

00:10:35.185 --> 00:10:36.560
On the other end
of the spectrum,

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:41.180
you get folks who sort
of live and breathe it.

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:43.150
And you've seen a
huge emphasis from all

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:45.160
the major front
offices in trying

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:49.570
to find ways to interject more
analytics into their decision

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:52.510
making, into their
player development,

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:54.340
and ultimately
trying to position

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:56.770
each player for success.

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:58.750
I think how that
actually manifests

00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:01.480
on an individual player
level varies quite a bit.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:06.680
Thank you.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:10.370
Jeff, I see you just put a
question in the chat as well.

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:14.595
Would you want to
ask that one live?

00:11:14.595 --> 00:11:18.030
Or I can read it.

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:23.276
Yeah, sorry, I'm on
baby duty right now.

00:11:23.276 --> 00:11:24.981
Hey Chase, long time.

00:11:24.981 --> 00:11:26.856
I was going to say,
long time no see, Jeff.

00:11:26.856 --> 00:11:27.750
Good to see you.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.750
Yeah, good to see you too.

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:35.870
I'm curious what the tech
stack looks like at the Cubs.

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:38.710
And is it different between--

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:40.750
I don't know the terminology
you're using-- but

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:42.140
like retail and professional?

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:44.537
Like the folks that are doing
the actual Moneyball stuff,

00:11:44.537 --> 00:11:46.120
are they using a
different tech stack?

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:49.630
And how is that different
from what we were

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:52.540
doing seven or eight years ago?

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:55.210
Yeah, it's a
good question, and

00:11:55.210 --> 00:11:57.565
awesome to see a friendly face.

00:11:57.565 --> 00:11:59.440
Yeah, for context, Jeff
and I worked together

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:01.780
for five or six years.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:06.590
And then we both gone
on our separate ways.

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:10.330
So briefly, so our tech
stack on the business side,

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:12.280
I think relatively standard.

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:16.210
We have a fairly robust data
warehouse infrastructure.

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:18.620
Currently, we're sitting
on an Oracle tech stack.

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:21.580
And we have-- our IT
team sort of formally

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:25.450
owns data ingestion,
all the ETL work.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:29.590
They also own our business
intelligence layer.

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:32.170
So in a lot of ways,
I sit on top of that.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:37.090
In my day-to-day, I have,
more specifically, RStudio

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:40.276
open pretty much
every single day.

00:12:40.276 --> 00:12:45.640
I run most of my
analytical work through R.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:47.950
On the baseball operations
side, to be honest,

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:51.100
we're almost two completely
separate companies.

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:55.030
I know they have a very
heavy R and Python base.

00:12:55.030 --> 00:12:58.780
They're also do a lot of
internal custom app development

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:00.470
work.

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:04.990
And so there's more of an app
development focus in that group

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:07.120
as well, that
they're finding ways

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.610
to marry the analytics
coming out of R or Python,

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:11.860
and then embedding
those into applications

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:14.365
that they can push out
to coaches and players.

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:21.210
Thanks, Jeff,
for your question.

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:24.150
And I did just want to say
if anybody writes a question

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:26.850
and you don't want me to
have you read it out loud

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:29.460
or if you're in like a
loud cafe or something,

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:31.470
you could put like a
little star at the end

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:34.900
and then I'll know
to just read it too.

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:37.760
But Ronald, I saw that you
had also asked a question

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:40.270
in the chat earlier.

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:43.378
Can I give you the mic and have
you add some context there too?

00:13:43.378 --> 00:13:44.170
RONALD: Yeah, sure.

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:47.980
I always like to
hear myself talk.

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:49.570
I am not a data analyst.

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:52.540
I just sort of play one at work.

00:13:52.540 --> 00:14:00.550
My question has to do with
how are your projects or areas

00:14:00.550 --> 00:14:03.220
of analysis determined.

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:06.370
And I ask that
because I find myself,

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:10.000
at least in this business,
working a little bit

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:13.600
with ad hoc where I have to
keep an eye on a lot of what

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:16.750
is going on in various
departments and area,

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:23.530
whether that's fiscal
or operations or patient

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:25.690
throughput or whatnot.

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:29.740
And I do get a certain
amount of guidance from,

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:32.170
oh, we have this
problem, solve it.

00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:33.880
But yet it's people
who really have

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:35.630
no idea how to go about that.

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:42.610
So actually doing a very
in-depth scope of work analysis

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:44.900
becomes critical.

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:47.410
And I'm wondering what
sort of a situation

00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:49.190
you've encountered there.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:49.690
Yeah--

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:51.607
RONALD: And this is a
really generic question.

00:14:51.607 --> 00:14:54.140
Just almost anybody
that I ask is like,

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:56.500
how does one do this optimally?

00:14:56.500 --> 00:15:00.100
Or is there an optimal way?

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:02.080
That's a really good question.

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:05.020
And it's something I spent
a ton of time thinking

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.210
through conceptually when
I joined the organization.

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:10.000
I was the first dedicated
business analytics hire.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.550
And so I was also
joining as a team of one,

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:17.720
so finite resource
in that respect.

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:20.290
I don't know if this
is best practice,

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:23.800
but what I ended up doing
is I essentially developed

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:27.460
three criteria and a
little checklist that

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:31.030
would allow me to have that
scoping exercise that you

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:32.680
referenced.

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:35.860
And really what that scoping
exercise boiled down to

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:37.750
is sort of, what's
the size of the prize

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:41.290
or what's the size
of the problem?

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:43.480
Two, do we actually
have data that

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:48.450
can help understand or
improve the problem?

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:50.200
And then three, when
does that-- when does

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:52.150
the work need to be done?

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:53.590
What's the timing?

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:56.830
And by focusing on
those three problems,

00:15:56.830 --> 00:16:01.360
I've gotten pretty adept
at filtering projects that

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:04.390
are material enough
that sort of warrant

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:06.400
a significant
investment of time.

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.410
Two, that we can
actually influence

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:10.840
through thinking about
through a data science

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:12.640
or an analytical perspective.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:15.250
And three, fits with
the business cadence.

00:16:15.250 --> 00:16:19.460
And if you strikeout or can't
check all three of those boxes,

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:22.420
then you can just facilitate
the next conversation

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:25.330
of like, OK, I can't
directly help you,

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:27.760
but how can we
move this forward.

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:32.200
And candidly, particularly
when I initially joined,

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:34.180
you have some
uncomfortable conversations

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:37.660
because as a service function
and a support resource,

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:40.780
having to tell your colleagues
that I can't directly help you

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:44.170
or here's why this
may not be a good fit,

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:48.310
those are tough
conversations to have.

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:51.880
But I think necessary
conversations

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:54.530
that you can prioritize
your time on driving data

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:55.290
through business.

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:01.790
Thanks, Chase.

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:05.270
And Libby, I see you just
asked a question in the chat

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:10.690
as well, if you want to
jump in and ask that one.

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:11.190
Sure.

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:15.569
Yeah, I was wondering about
dynamic, like real-time dynamic

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:16.957
pricing models for tickets.

00:17:16.957 --> 00:17:19.290
That's something that I've
always been really interested

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:21.420
in as a person who does
like optimization and stuff

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:22.630
like that.

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:24.750
But I don't know how
widespread that actually is.

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:27.359
And I was wondering
how the pandemic

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:31.320
might have shifted some of
those models for sports teams.

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:35.490
Yeah, so dynamic
pricing, it's a big topic

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:37.920
and a huge area of focus.

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:43.050
As an organization, I believe we
began adopting dynamic pricing

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:44.910
in 2011 or 2012.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:49.800
So we've got roughly a decade
sort of under our belt.

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:52.500
I will say I would draw a big
distinction in how we think

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:54.810
about dynamic pricing
versus someone

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:57.570
like a leading e-commerce
store, someone like Amazon

00:17:57.570 --> 00:17:59.640
where they've got
millions of SKUs

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:02.010
and they have some
humans behind the scenes,

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:04.950
but they've deployed
machine learning

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:07.350
models to sort of
algorithmically price

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:12.250
and manipulate prices in a
somewhat automated fashion.

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:14.890
We're not that hands-off.

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:17.940
So behind the scenes there's
a collaboration between myself

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:19.440
and our ticketing colleagues.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.210
And we're, essentially
in real time,

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:28.110
always monitoring and observing
demand as it materializes.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:29.760
We have some
analytical models that

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:36.750
help us make some estimates
on how we may change price.

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:41.467
But it is a little more manual
and hands on than, at least

00:18:41.467 --> 00:18:43.050
in an academic
setting, when you think

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:46.080
about this is the
optimal dynamic pricing

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:49.560
infrastructure where we've got
some real headroom to sort of

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:53.812
grow into that type of model.

00:18:53.812 --> 00:18:54.770
I think that's great.

00:18:54.770 --> 00:18:55.270
Thank you.

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:57.500
Yeah, I think keeping
the human in the loop,

00:18:57.500 --> 00:19:00.320
like the algorithm loop,
is really interesting.

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:03.738
And also kind of the
algorithm aversion

00:19:03.738 --> 00:19:05.280
that you might see
where you're like,

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:07.290
oh, I don't think that's
the right thing to do.

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:10.250
I don't want the computer
to make the decision for me.

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:11.960
That's been an
interesting topic.

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:15.170
And I think that it's great that
you guys use it as a reference

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:17.810
and not as something
that's automatic.

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:19.640
Yeah, so part
of this, I think,

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:23.450
is the sports
industry write our--

00:19:23.450 --> 00:19:25.880
there's two dynamics that
sort of distinguish us

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:28.100
from a lot of other dynamic
pricing applications.

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:30.410
One, we have finite inventory.

00:19:30.410 --> 00:19:34.100
Got 41,000 seats per event.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:36.890
And two, that inventory
expires and becomes perishable.

00:19:36.890 --> 00:19:42.260
So I can't sell the ticket
to last Tuesday's game.

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:46.280
And then the third part of
that is, in addition, we

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:48.260
have multiple sales channels.

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:52.310
And so accurately, you can
get some interesting signals

00:19:52.310 --> 00:19:54.740
in your data that may or
may not actually correlate

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:56.560
to an increase or
decrease in demand.

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:59.300
So for example, a large
group ticket order,

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:01.280
if there is a church
group or some--

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:05.000
we may sell a chunk
of 500 tickets.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:07.670
Without some proper
treatment and sort

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:10.640
of nuanced understanding
of how that data actually

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:12.890
flows through our
systems, you could

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:16.790
make the naive interpretation
of demand is through the roof.

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:18.110
Don't change price.

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.390
And that may or may
not actually have

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:22.610
anything to do with
the underlying demand.

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:25.340
It's just like when a group
sale goes through, for example.

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:31.110
I have a similar
question to Robert,

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:33.870
who just put in the chat,
should we use incognito mode

00:20:33.870 --> 00:20:36.195
when purchasing sports tickets?

00:20:36.195 --> 00:20:37.820
I've always wondered
that for concerts.

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:42.920
I mean, I would say this
is more my personal bias.

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:44.628
I mean, you should
probably use incognito

00:20:44.628 --> 00:20:47.400
for most of the things that
you do on the internet.

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:51.300
And I would say that probably
extends to purchasing sports

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:53.040
tickets.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:56.700
No, I mean, I think both through
our official primary channel,

00:20:56.700 --> 00:21:03.510
you know forbes.com/tickets, we
have a decently robust ad tech

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:07.050
stack behind the scenes that
will allow us to sort of track

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:12.990
your behavior to some extent
and then show you relevant ads

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:14.460
or sort of follow up to that.

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:17.910
You'll certainly, if you look at
the secondary market, StubHub,

00:21:17.910 --> 00:21:21.090
Vivid, [INAUDIBLE],,
those organizations

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:25.350
have very robust ad
tech stacks that I'm

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:28.590
somewhat envious
of because that's

00:21:28.590 --> 00:21:30.300
central to the success
of their business.

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:35.070
And they've made the
necessary investments.

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:39.830
So can you tell us like
buy tickets x days before?

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:41.570
Just kidding.

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:43.460
No, I mean, that's
an interesting one.

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:47.630
People do-- we have seen
a huge shift of people

00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:50.780
delaying their purchase
closer and closer to events.

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:54.018
And so that's a consumer
behavior phenomenon

00:21:54.018 --> 00:21:56.060
that we're seeing across
all sorts of industries.

00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:58.250
I think that creates a
risk for us as a business,

00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:01.360
going back to my point
related to expiring inventory.

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:06.290
So as sales get pushed closer
and closer to the event date,

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:09.290
that just creates more
uncertainty for our business,

00:22:09.290 --> 00:22:13.070
and where we spend a lot
of time thinking about

00:22:13.070 --> 00:22:15.200
how do we operate
in this environment

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:16.325
that we're watching unfold.

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:20.190
Right.

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:21.900
I see an anonymous
question that just

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:24.210
came in that was, since
sports games happened

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:27.810
so frequently, how are models
and analytical projects

00:22:27.810 --> 00:22:30.120
evaluated over what time period?

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:37.590
It's highly context specific.

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:41.650
In baseball, there's
a couple of lenses

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:44.680
that most of our decisions
sort of naturally fall into.

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:48.100
One is a yearly cadence.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:50.770
To some extent, the
baseball schedule

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:54.000
is pretty much sentence
set in stone from April 1

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:55.970
to end of September.

00:22:55.970 --> 00:22:59.770
And so a lot of the work that we
do, the true and sort of final

00:22:59.770 --> 00:23:03.323
evaluation for success
certainly sort of materializes

00:23:03.323 --> 00:23:04.240
throughout the season.

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:08.080
But the real evaluation is
after the season has unfolded.

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:10.270
More at the operational
level, there

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:14.650
is success that can be
evaluated and changes actually

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:18.880
put in practice, sometimes
on a game to game basis,

00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:23.540
but more often than not, a
home stand by home stand basis.

00:23:23.540 --> 00:23:26.150
And so it's roughly
every other week.

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:29.008
So we can make
operational changes.

00:23:29.008 --> 00:23:31.300
The team goes away for 10
days and then they come back,

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:33.700
and so we can make some
changes in between home stands.

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:38.840
Thank you.

00:23:38.840 --> 00:23:41.300
Mubanga, I see you had,
I think, a follow-up

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:44.300
question maybe to what
Libby had asked earlier.

00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:46.100
Would you want to
jump in and ask that?

00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:58.670
Or we can get back
to that one after.

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:01.880
I wasn't sure how to summarize
the question in there.

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:06.050
But I see there's also an
anonymous question for you,

00:24:06.050 --> 00:24:07.050
Chase that was--

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:09.140
Sorry, Rachel, I was on mute.

00:24:09.140 --> 00:24:15.110
It was basically a comment
based on what Chase explained

00:24:15.110 --> 00:24:18.830
in reaction to Libby's
question because I was just

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:22.970
thinking to myself, because
the pricing is dynamic

00:24:22.970 --> 00:24:28.460
so it means that the system is
changing continuously in time.

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:31.220
So I've seen applications before
where reinforcement learning

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:33.050
has been used in--

00:24:33.050 --> 00:24:34.830
it's not exactly
the same setting,

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:38.940
but in settings like that, like
self-driving cars for example.

00:24:38.940 --> 00:24:42.080
So I was just wondering to
myself, what kind of algorithm

00:24:42.080 --> 00:24:44.510
they use at the Cubs?

00:24:48.290 --> 00:24:52.050
So I would say--

00:24:52.050 --> 00:24:55.650
so specific to
dynamic pricing, we

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:59.670
have tested a wide range
of different algorithms

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:03.060
and sort of decision frameworks.

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:06.060
Everything from your sort
of traditional, classic

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:11.490
regression-based models
to non-parametric models.

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:15.210
Jeff and I, in our prior life,
worked at a consulting shop

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:18.600
that specialized in a variety
of Bayesian techniques

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:22.410
and so I've sort of
messed around and tried

00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:25.380
to make sort of the
Bayesian frameworks fit

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:29.610
within the type of
data that we have.

00:25:29.610 --> 00:25:34.950
I will say, having spent a fair
amount of time in that area,

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:38.930
I think the bigger challenge
that we have is more

00:25:38.930 --> 00:25:42.650
at the data synthesis
and aggregation layer,

00:25:42.650 --> 00:25:46.580
getting the various data
sets that you would want

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:50.090
in real time or sort of at the
right granularity or frequency

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:52.520
that we ideally want
to make decisions.

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:54.140
We run into more
bottlenecks at that

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:57.500
layer versus any
individual algorithm

00:25:57.500 --> 00:26:00.988
will end up being somewhat
performant given the data

00:26:00.988 --> 00:26:02.030
constraints that we have.

00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:04.970
So I spend more of my
time focused at, how

00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:10.940
can I go get the incremental
data at a more fine granularity

00:26:10.940 --> 00:26:12.890
so that we can sort
of more meaningfully

00:26:12.890 --> 00:26:17.750
engage in more of the sort of
true optimization, algorithm

00:26:17.750 --> 00:26:18.530
optimization.

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:24.380
Thanks, chase.

00:26:24.380 --> 00:26:27.140
I see Jeff asked another
question in the chat that was,

00:26:27.140 --> 00:26:30.800
is there any animosity
between the Cubs and resellers

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:31.970
like Ticketmaster?

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:39.130
It's a good question.

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:42.130
So in the ticket
distribution space,

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:44.050
you've got a couple
different entities.

00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:47.770
You have us as the Cubs and we
sell on our own channels that

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:50.730
you go to the box office, you
can call a telephone number,

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:52.090
cubs.com/tickets.

00:26:52.090 --> 00:26:54.640
Then you have the
secondary markets.

00:26:54.640 --> 00:26:56.800
But you also have the--

00:26:56.800 --> 00:27:00.070
different terms are used-- but
brokers are essentially people

00:27:00.070 --> 00:27:03.680
who are in the business of
acquiring inventory from us

00:27:03.680 --> 00:27:08.140
and then selling those
tickets in a variety--

00:27:08.140 --> 00:27:10.150
through a variety of channels.

00:27:10.150 --> 00:27:13.990
I think there is some
healthy tension that

00:27:13.990 --> 00:27:18.700
can arise because the incentives
that each of those parties

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:23.150
may not always align
with one another.

00:27:23.150 --> 00:27:27.520
And so in general, I
think our incentives

00:27:27.520 --> 00:27:30.880
are pretty aligned with the
secondary changers themselves.

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:34.540
We want to have sort of the
broadest distribution pipes

00:27:34.540 --> 00:27:38.230
that we can and get our
tickets in front of people

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:42.370
wherever they are and however
they want to purchase.

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:45.310
Those exchanges, they're
obviously interested in helping

00:27:45.310 --> 00:27:46.270
to sell Cubs tickets.

00:27:46.270 --> 00:27:49.570
But they have a huge portfolio
of live event inventory

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:52.000
that they also care about.

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:53.400
So there's just--

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:55.150
I don't think we've
perfected the business

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:57.400
model or the
relationships between all

00:27:57.400 --> 00:27:59.530
of the various parties.

00:27:59.530 --> 00:28:03.115
But in general, we work
pretty well together.

00:28:07.770 --> 00:28:08.270
Thank you.

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:12.810
And apologies, my audio just got
