00:29:37.310 --> 00:29:39.320
much purview into.

00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:42.830
Certainly collaborative
like board materials,

00:29:42.830 --> 00:29:47.380
if we're putting a board
deck or a memo together,

00:29:47.380 --> 00:29:48.800
need to provide
deliverables that

00:29:48.800 --> 00:29:52.130
can easily be dropped and
live in those documents.

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:57.510
Thanks.

00:29:57.510 --> 00:29:59.790
Can I dive a bit
deeper there because I'm

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:01.830
sure that a bunch of
people on the call

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:05.400
have that struggle as well.

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:09.480
How are you going about
trying to change that

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:15.120
or like working to
gather the team?

00:30:15.120 --> 00:30:17.560
I'm not sure I
have a great answer.

00:30:17.560 --> 00:30:21.240
I think, internally, I am
making a conscious effort

00:30:21.240 --> 00:30:23.580
to shift more and more
of our deliverables

00:30:23.580 --> 00:30:29.280
into more reproducible, R
Markdown, or just sort of fully

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:33.750
contained scripts and
processes and then

00:30:33.750 --> 00:30:35.550
sort of go kicking
and screaming when

00:30:35.550 --> 00:30:38.820
we need to break that paradigm.

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:44.670
But it's a constant sort
of tension or something

00:30:44.670 --> 00:30:46.230
that we're always evaluating.

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:52.110
So with R Markdown
does the team also

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:57.660
generate PowerPoint or
Word from R Markdown too?

00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:59.940
I tried, to be honest, given--

00:30:59.940 --> 00:31:04.050
and this is sort of in the
weeds on the specific formats.

00:31:04.050 --> 00:31:10.590
And for the preferences that
we have in those templates,

00:31:10.590 --> 00:31:13.230
I haven't been able
to fully close the gap

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:16.740
and get 100% continuity there.

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:19.410
I'm sure it could be done.

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:22.410
It's more of a timing
and resourcing thing.

00:31:22.410 --> 00:31:25.770
Yeah, that's something I
hear a lot from like the Excel

00:31:25.770 --> 00:31:31.800
reports perspective too, like
around certain formatting.

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.260
Tatiana, I see you had
a question you just

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:35.760
asked in the chat as well.

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:39.050
Would you want to
ask that one live?

00:31:39.050 --> 00:31:40.340
Sure.

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:43.670
So you did mention,
Chase, that you

00:31:43.670 --> 00:31:46.250
deal with ambiguity
and uncertainty a lot.

00:31:46.250 --> 00:31:49.550
So I'd be curious to hear what
are your most go-to techniques

00:31:49.550 --> 00:31:50.720
for approaching ambiguity.

00:31:50.720 --> 00:31:52.920
And where you basically
stop and say, hey,

00:31:52.920 --> 00:31:55.340
this is the threshold where
I'm about to accept it

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:57.350
and I'm not going
to dig further?

00:31:57.350 --> 00:31:59.660
And the second question was
just about your journey.

00:31:59.660 --> 00:32:03.290
Since you started on as
a one man department,

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:04.520
how did your role evolve?

00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:07.915
And what aspect
did you enjoy most?

00:32:07.915 --> 00:32:09.540
From business analysis
to data science,

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:11.920
it seems like you're more
into data science right now,

00:32:11.920 --> 00:32:14.010
and I'm curious to
hear how it started.

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:16.330
Yeah, both good questions.

00:32:16.330 --> 00:32:19.210
I'll try-- I'll
take them in order.

00:32:19.210 --> 00:32:24.150
So in thinking
about uncertainty,

00:32:24.150 --> 00:32:25.770
I think as an organization--

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:27.810
I think there's two
questions-- there are two

00:32:27.810 --> 00:32:29.220
questions embedded in there.

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:33.450
One is more the
mechanically, how

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.720
do you think about and actually
quantify the uncertainty

00:32:36.720 --> 00:32:39.640
from an analytical perspective?

00:32:39.640 --> 00:32:42.900
And I'd go back to a lot
of the Bayesian frameworks

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:45.510
that I was introduced
to in my first job

00:32:45.510 --> 00:32:51.630
or they've just got some nice
academically rigorous and just

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:54.060
like conceptually sound
managers to actually think

00:32:54.060 --> 00:32:56.370
about confidence intervals
or credible intervals.

00:32:56.370 --> 00:33:00.810
And so I use those frameworks
sort of mechanically.

00:33:00.810 --> 00:33:03.060
I think where I have
spent a lot of time

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:05.070
is getting my colleagues
and counterparts

00:33:05.070 --> 00:33:07.770
in our organization
comfortable with embracing

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:10.050
that uncertainty.

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:14.110
A lot of the areas
where I spend my time,

00:33:14.110 --> 00:33:16.980
we have to-- our
analytical deliverables are

00:33:16.980 --> 00:33:20.430
do several weeks or months
in advance of those events

00:33:20.430 --> 00:33:22.590
actually happening.

00:33:22.590 --> 00:33:25.720
And to sit here in the
first week of November

00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:29.580
and with 100% specificity
say, here's my projection

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:34.160
for the ticket sales next
July, it's really hard

00:33:34.160 --> 00:33:35.810
to make that argument.

00:33:35.810 --> 00:33:38.120
But I've spent a lot of time
getting our organization

00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:40.892
to understand, like here's the
range of probable outcomes.

00:33:40.892 --> 00:33:42.350
Here are the things
that would make

00:33:42.350 --> 00:33:45.980
us believe that we may
over-index or sort of

00:33:45.980 --> 00:33:47.390
underperform.

00:33:47.390 --> 00:33:52.430
And how much risk or uncertainty
are we willing to accept?

00:33:52.430 --> 00:33:56.090
And what's the downside
of if we misproject

00:33:56.090 --> 00:33:59.900
or basically what's the
cost of being wrong?

00:33:59.900 --> 00:34:02.810
And that's taken the better
part of my five years

00:34:02.810 --> 00:34:08.000
here to get folks
to fully embrace.

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:10.370
COVID-19 was somewhat
beneficial in that

00:34:10.370 --> 00:34:11.960
because we had so
much uncertainty

00:34:11.960 --> 00:34:13.489
facing our business.

00:34:13.489 --> 00:34:15.980
That uncertainty, it
was front and center.

00:34:15.980 --> 00:34:18.409
And there was no other way,
at least in my opinion,

00:34:18.409 --> 00:34:21.469
to like reasonably think about
what may happen in our business

00:34:21.469 --> 00:34:25.130
than through sort of a
truly probabilistic manner.

00:34:27.739 --> 00:34:28.239
I'm sorry.

00:34:28.239 --> 00:34:32.030
Your second question
was more on--

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:35.060
And on how your role
basically evolved.

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:37.199
But going back to
uncertainty for a second.

00:34:37.199 --> 00:34:40.190
So you basically, you
first built out your model,

00:34:40.190 --> 00:34:41.870
and then you accept
stakeholders?

00:34:41.870 --> 00:34:44.177
So you consult them on
what level of uncertainty

00:34:44.177 --> 00:34:45.260
they're willing to accept?

00:34:45.260 --> 00:34:47.593
Or do you set it beforehand
and you have a conversation,

00:34:47.593 --> 00:34:49.520
like, here's my data.

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:53.280
Here's how far we can go.

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:56.740
And here's the levels of
uncertainty we can dig into.

00:34:56.740 --> 00:34:58.490
Is that a conversation
you have beforehand

00:34:58.490 --> 00:35:01.730
or after you build your model?

00:35:01.730 --> 00:35:03.530
Or it depends?

00:35:03.530 --> 00:35:05.315
To some extent, it depends.

00:35:09.980 --> 00:35:13.310
Before materially
engaging with colleagues,

00:35:13.310 --> 00:35:16.850
I try and sort of push
the analytical models

00:35:16.850 --> 00:35:17.700
as far as we can.

00:35:17.700 --> 00:35:21.500
So I have an understanding
on here's how much

00:35:21.500 --> 00:35:23.330
sort of statistical
power, how confident we

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:24.350
can be in these results.

00:35:24.350 --> 00:35:28.730
And then also framing
up we are getting

00:35:28.730 --> 00:35:30.890
into the grounds
of like, we don't

00:35:30.890 --> 00:35:32.120
have specific enough data.

00:35:32.120 --> 00:35:34.010
Or there's just
more uncertainty.

00:35:34.010 --> 00:35:36.080
We're sort of crossing
a threshold of we're

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:40.290
asking for precision that this
model can't really support.

00:35:40.290 --> 00:35:42.320
So let's take a step back
and sort of understand

00:35:42.320 --> 00:35:45.360
what those limits are.

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:46.890
Does that answer your question?

00:35:46.890 --> 00:35:47.910
Yeah, kind of does.

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:50.440
Because it's hard to answer
a big question, right?

00:35:50.440 --> 00:35:52.468
So yeah.

00:35:52.468 --> 00:35:54.510
And the second question
was about just your role,

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:55.620
how it evolved.

00:35:55.620 --> 00:36:00.630
I assume that since you were
the first person, right,

00:36:00.630 --> 00:36:04.470
as far as I understand it
in your analytics department

00:36:04.470 --> 00:36:06.150
in your company,
how much did you

00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:11.310
have to spend on the buy in, and
business analysis, et cetera?

00:36:11.310 --> 00:36:17.820
Or if the stage the company was
in when you joined them was I

00:36:17.820 --> 00:36:21.300
guess cognizant enough
of the processes,

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:23.550
so you didn't have to
spend a lot of time

00:36:23.550 --> 00:36:25.320
on educating I guess?

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:28.560
And which aspect did you enjoy
most as your role evolved?

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:31.860
Yeah, when I joined,
the organization

00:36:31.860 --> 00:36:34.800
had recognized the need to
bring on some more resources

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:38.080
and in the business
analytics area.

00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:40.170
So there was a little
bit of initial buy-in

00:36:40.170 --> 00:36:46.620
that had already occurred that
led to my role being created.

00:36:46.620 --> 00:36:49.050
Having said that,
in any organization,

00:36:49.050 --> 00:36:51.610
you're ultimately building
relationships with individuals.

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:55.740
And so I was pretty conscious
in finding some quick wins,

00:36:55.740 --> 00:36:59.250
and sort of early
victories that I

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:01.740
could find to gain
that credibility,

00:37:01.740 --> 00:37:07.260
so that we could grow
into bigger, more complex,

00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:10.080
and challenging problems.

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:12.900
When I joined, I'd say
most of the problems

00:37:12.900 --> 00:37:15.480
that I initially worked on
in the first 12 to 18 months

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:17.790
were sort of pretty
pure point analytics.

00:37:17.790 --> 00:37:20.210
Like, the problems are
well-defined and scoped.

00:37:20.210 --> 00:37:23.130
There's a clear
analytical angle that we

00:37:23.130 --> 00:37:27.480
could bring to the table,
but it was pretty well boxed.

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:30.090
As I have progressed
through my five years here,

00:37:30.090 --> 00:37:32.880
more and more of the
work that I spend

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:35.730
on where I spend my
time now is addressing

00:37:35.730 --> 00:37:39.090
bigger, strategic issues for
the organization, and sort

00:37:39.090 --> 00:37:41.490
of somewhat major sort
of policy decisions

00:37:41.490 --> 00:37:43.470
that we are evaluating.

00:37:43.470 --> 00:37:50.160
And I certainly own, and sort of
bring the consumer and the data

00:37:50.160 --> 00:37:51.360
perspective to the table.

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:56.130
But there are very real sort
of non-quantitative other

00:37:56.130 --> 00:37:57.960
stakeholders, and
other considerations

00:37:57.960 --> 00:38:00.120
that as an organization
we need to evaluate.

00:38:00.120 --> 00:38:04.990
And we have a very large media
presence as an organization.

00:38:04.990 --> 00:38:08.040
And so we're very
conscious of the messages

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:11.490
that we put into the market,
and sort of our standing

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:13.597
just from a PR standpoint.

00:38:13.597 --> 00:38:15.180
I've not figured out
how to weave that

00:38:15.180 --> 00:38:17.580
into my analytical models.

00:38:17.580 --> 00:38:20.790
But it's a very
real consideration

00:38:20.790 --> 00:38:24.270
that it's more of an open
discussion and dialogue

00:38:24.270 --> 00:38:26.250
with my colleagues.

00:38:29.610 --> 00:38:31.080
Thank you.

00:38:31.080 --> 00:38:33.820
I see Robert had
also asked earlier,

00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:37.170
what does collaboration look
like with various vendors

00:38:37.170 --> 00:38:40.500
that the Cubs uses?

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:43.500
And then Libby kind of had a
plus one to that that asks,

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:47.730
are vendors connected in any way
as far as data sharing, like,

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:51.700
from a connected supply
chain perspective?

00:38:51.700 --> 00:38:53.963
So I guess--

00:38:53.963 --> 00:38:55.630
Libby, do you mind
helping me understand

00:38:55.630 --> 00:38:59.560
the second part of your question
in a little bit more detail?

00:38:59.560 --> 00:39:02.410
I could go in a couple of
different directions here.

00:39:02.410 --> 00:39:02.950
LIBBY: Sure.

00:39:02.950 --> 00:39:06.400
So I was wondering
if the Cubs have

00:39:06.400 --> 00:39:10.270
visibility into the data
of suppliers or vendors?

00:39:10.270 --> 00:39:13.070
And if that goes the
other direction as well,

00:39:13.070 --> 00:39:16.050
if it's even present?

00:39:16.050 --> 00:39:17.838
I guess-- sorry, I
keep diving in here.

00:39:17.838 --> 00:39:19.380
Are you thinking
sort of specifically

00:39:19.380 --> 00:39:25.260
in consumer type data, or more
other operational business data

00:39:25.260 --> 00:39:27.743
that we need to create?

00:39:27.743 --> 00:39:29.160
LIBBY: So I would
say a little bit

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:31.650
of both, whether
that's information

00:39:31.650 --> 00:39:35.190
about your consumers that are
coming from vendors who might

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:38.280
be servicing ticket
sales, or if it's

00:39:38.280 --> 00:39:42.240
more of a supply type thing,
where you guys have business

00:39:42.240 --> 00:39:42.930
suppliers.

00:39:42.930 --> 00:39:46.560
Do they have visibility
into your supply chain?

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:48.660
Or do you have visibility
upstream or downstream

00:39:48.660 --> 00:39:50.830
in that sort of
sense of the word?

00:39:50.830 --> 00:39:51.330
Yeah, OK.

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:53.590
Thank you, super helpful.

00:39:53.590 --> 00:39:57.180
So I guess a couple
parts of that,

00:39:57.180 --> 00:40:01.920
the main driver of customer
data are ticket sales.

00:40:01.920 --> 00:40:04.050
So we have sort of
complete visibility

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:06.490
into the ticket sale process.

00:40:09.210 --> 00:40:13.950
Four or five years ago, we began
delivering all of our tickets

00:40:13.950 --> 00:40:14.530
mobily.

00:40:14.530 --> 00:40:17.123
So you can no longer
print a ticket.

00:40:17.123 --> 00:40:18.540
You don't get a
hard stack ticket.

00:40:18.540 --> 00:40:22.180
You can't do the print
a PDF thing anymore.

00:40:22.180 --> 00:40:25.870
So through that, we are
generating a lot of data.

00:40:25.870 --> 00:40:27.330
So now, we get
ticket forward data.

00:40:27.330 --> 00:40:31.410
And so we get more
insight into sort of who

00:40:31.410 --> 00:40:34.710
is actually attending a game.

00:40:34.710 --> 00:40:38.190
And the next evolution
of that that we

00:40:38.190 --> 00:40:39.930
unrolled for 2020
rollout is through

00:40:39.930 --> 00:40:42.330
our global ordering footprint.

00:40:42.330 --> 00:40:45.000
Now, we can actually
tie individual--

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:48.630
now, we've got pretty good
insight into the ticket data.

00:40:48.630 --> 00:40:52.590
Now, for the people who leverage
our mobile ordering platform,

00:40:52.590 --> 00:40:54.960
now I can actually start to
understand food and beverage

00:40:54.960 --> 00:40:57.360
preferences about that
individual which we've never

00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:59.250
had insight into before.

00:40:59.250 --> 00:41:01.530
Historically, you'd go
to the concession stand,

00:41:01.530 --> 00:41:02.820
you'd buy whatever you buy.

00:41:02.820 --> 00:41:04.778
We'd get like the last
four of the credit card,

00:41:04.778 --> 00:41:09.840
but no meaningful way to tie
that back to any individual.

00:41:09.840 --> 00:41:13.260
So that's been sort of
the main sources of data.

00:41:13.260 --> 00:41:17.510
We also our own WiFi deployment,
so we generate some customer

00:41:17.510 --> 00:41:19.640
data there.

00:41:19.640 --> 00:41:23.990
We do have-- obviously,
we have a decent footprint

00:41:23.990 --> 00:41:27.410
in terms of digital spend across
the social media websites.

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:29.480
And so we kind of
fit into probably

00:41:29.480 --> 00:41:32.750
they're like small
medium business category.

00:41:32.750 --> 00:41:35.930
But we have active engagement
and collaboration with them

00:41:35.930 --> 00:41:41.758
to improve our marketing
operations basically.

00:41:45.350 --> 00:41:46.790
Thanks, Chase.

00:41:46.790 --> 00:41:49.760
I know that some of the
R in sports analytics

00:41:49.760 --> 00:41:53.720
meet-ups a lot of people have
questions around how to get

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:56.600
that first job in sports.

00:41:56.600 --> 00:41:59.900
I'm curious to hear your
perspective there on maybe

00:41:59.900 --> 00:42:04.110
how you did that, or how you'd
advise others to go about that.

00:42:04.110 --> 00:42:07.700
So I think finding
the first job in sports

00:42:07.700 --> 00:42:09.620
is often the most
challenging one.

00:42:09.620 --> 00:42:14.247
I think once you've found
a foothold, particularly

00:42:14.247 --> 00:42:15.830
on the business side,
it's pretty open

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:17.610
and sort of
collaborative community.

00:42:17.610 --> 00:42:21.230
And so you see a fair
amount of not job hopping,

00:42:21.230 --> 00:42:25.760
but you can sort of navigate
the professional team landscape

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:26.480
somewhat.

00:42:26.480 --> 00:42:29.780
I won't say usually, but
it happens quite a bit.

00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:32.600
In terms of putting
together a strategy

00:42:32.600 --> 00:42:34.600
to land that first
job in sports,

00:42:34.600 --> 00:42:37.650
I'd highlight a
couple of things.

00:42:37.650 --> 00:42:40.210
One, I go back to sort
of where I started.

00:42:40.210 --> 00:42:42.110
If your preference
and priority is

00:42:42.110 --> 00:42:46.550
to work in sort of a baseball,
or the player operations

00:42:46.550 --> 00:42:49.530
side of the house, there's
a pretty clear path

00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:53.900
that developing some
expertise in being incredibly

00:42:53.900 --> 00:42:55.910
smart, and sophisticated,
and understanding

00:42:55.910 --> 00:42:58.490
player performance.

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:00.688
On the business side,
at least for me,

00:43:00.688 --> 00:43:03.230
I had really no understanding
of how the business of baseball

00:43:03.230 --> 00:43:05.550
worked before I got
into the business.

00:43:05.550 --> 00:43:08.000
But there's some good
publicly available resources

00:43:08.000 --> 00:43:10.010
for not only baseball,
but all the major sports.

00:43:10.010 --> 00:43:13.970
Forbes does a really good job
of just synthesizing here's

00:43:13.970 --> 00:43:17.010
how the business side of
these operations work.

00:43:17.010 --> 00:43:19.970
And so I spent some
time just level

00:43:19.970 --> 00:43:21.830
setting, and really
understanding

00:43:21.830 --> 00:43:25.100
how businesses operate.

00:43:25.100 --> 00:43:28.460
The second focus I have is, you
need to get a little scrappy

00:43:28.460 --> 00:43:31.520
and tap into your
network, and find

00:43:31.520 --> 00:43:35.400
friends, or friends of
friends, reach out on LinkedIn.

00:43:35.400 --> 00:43:38.960
Or if you Google Cubs front
office, or any team front

00:43:38.960 --> 00:43:43.777
office, you'll in general, find
a whole roster of employees.

00:43:43.777 --> 00:43:45.110
So look at the job descriptions.

00:43:45.110 --> 00:43:45.902
Look at the titles.

00:43:45.902 --> 00:43:47.120
Look at the individuals.

00:43:47.120 --> 00:43:52.880
Reach out, and start to
build some connections.

00:43:52.880 --> 00:43:56.750
The third one is, be patient.

00:43:56.750 --> 00:43:59.550
All front offices are smaller
than maybe most people

00:43:59.550 --> 00:44:00.050
would think.

00:44:00.050 --> 00:44:02.090
And so jobs don't
have necessarily

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:05.640
had a predictable
hiring cadence.

00:44:05.640 --> 00:44:09.230
And so be patient
and do some diligence

00:44:09.230 --> 00:44:13.160
to figure out when jobs
do become available.

00:44:13.160 --> 00:44:17.630
And the last thing
I'd highlight is,

00:44:17.630 --> 00:44:19.958
if your heart is set on
working for a front office,

00:44:19.958 --> 00:44:22.500
I'm certainly not going to tell
you to not go down that path.

00:44:22.500 --> 00:44:25.430
But there are some
awesome jobs available.

00:44:25.430 --> 00:44:28.970
And when I say the front
office, adjacent industries,

00:44:28.970 --> 00:44:34.520
the major concessionaires
all employ

00:44:34.520 --> 00:44:37.760
but hundreds of analytics
focused professionals.

00:44:37.760 --> 00:44:41.090
There's a great sort of
cottage industry of consultants

00:44:41.090 --> 00:44:42.960
that focus on the sports space.

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:46.160
So depending on your
preferences and priorities,

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:53.050
you can land a job in any
number of sport adjacent areas.

00:44:53.050 --> 00:44:56.710
So the last thing I'll say is,
it's a little bit self-serving

00:44:56.710 --> 00:44:57.210
here.

00:44:57.210 --> 00:45:02.023
But I am looking to add a
couple of folks to my team.

00:45:02.023 --> 00:45:03.690
We got one job
description up right now,

00:45:03.690 --> 00:45:05.530
and another will be up shortly.

00:45:05.530 --> 00:45:10.260
So if anyone's interested
in focusing on improving

00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:13.790
the business operations,
cubs.com/jobs.

00:45:13.790 --> 00:45:16.380
And there, we've got
some opportunities

00:45:16.380 --> 00:45:19.640
available on our team.

00:45:19.640 --> 00:45:21.320
Awesome.

00:45:21.320 --> 00:45:25.130
Something that might be helpful
to dive into deeper here,

00:45:25.130 --> 00:45:26.780
and maybe with the
whole group as well

00:45:26.780 --> 00:45:29.750
is, when we talk about
being able to find

00:45:29.750 --> 00:45:32.750
these opportunities
and networking.

00:45:32.750 --> 00:45:37.340
I wonder how finding a
diverse pool of candidates

00:45:37.340 --> 00:45:38.660
also plays into that.

00:45:38.660 --> 00:45:42.750
And what are some of the things
that your organization does?

00:45:42.750 --> 00:45:43.250
Yeah.

00:45:46.400 --> 00:45:47.800
It's a challenge.

00:45:52.170 --> 00:45:56.910
We're making a very conscious
effort to try and raise

00:45:56.910 --> 00:46:02.340
the brand awareness and profile
of our organization to groups

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:08.730
who may not otherwise
naturally find cubs.com/jobs.

00:46:08.730 --> 00:46:11.160
The tactics on how
you execute that

00:46:11.160 --> 00:46:15.510
are somewhat specific
to the individual roles.

00:46:15.510 --> 00:46:21.420
But it's a big emphasis
and priority for us

00:46:21.420 --> 00:46:26.040
as an organization
that I think we had--

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:28.950
it's just an area that we
can get better and evolve.

00:46:28.950 --> 00:46:31.140
And so it's making
conscious efforts

00:46:31.140 --> 00:46:33.810
to increase sort of
the top of the funnel,

00:46:33.810 --> 00:46:36.870
how do we raise our brand
awareness and footprint,

00:46:36.870 --> 00:46:41.700
so that we can attract a more
qualified and diverse group

00:46:41.700 --> 00:46:43.770
of candidates.

00:46:43.770 --> 00:46:48.170
It's something that we're
spending a lot of time trying

00:46:48.170 --> 00:46:50.960
to evolving it better at.

00:46:54.155 --> 00:46:54.780
That's great.

00:46:54.780 --> 00:46:57.020
Thank you.

00:46:57.020 --> 00:46:59.990
I see there was another
anonymous question

00:46:59.990 --> 00:47:04.190
earlier that asked about
what you originally studied.

00:47:04.190 --> 00:47:05.270
Was it analytics?

00:47:05.270 --> 00:47:09.500
Or how did you get
into this space?

00:47:09.500 --> 00:47:12.680
So my undergrad work
focused on political science

00:47:12.680 --> 00:47:17.510
and psychology, so not terribly
heavy on the data analytics

00:47:17.510 --> 00:47:18.890
front.

00:47:18.890 --> 00:47:22.880
My first job, I feel like I got
the equivalent of a master's

00:47:22.880 --> 00:47:26.660
in applied statistics, a
lot of on the job training.

00:47:26.660 --> 00:47:30.170
I supplemented that with
some graduate coursework

00:47:30.170 --> 00:47:31.250
in statistics.

00:47:31.250 --> 00:47:35.240
And then I went back
and did my MBA part time

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:38.960
with a focus on operations,
analytics, and marketing.

00:47:38.960 --> 00:47:43.730
But I think most of my practical
what I use in my day-to-day

00:47:43.730 --> 00:47:46.820
really came from my first job.

00:47:46.820 --> 00:47:49.550
And are there-- so are
there certain skills that

00:47:49.550 --> 00:47:52.910
you're hiring for right
now, or certain things that

00:47:52.910 --> 00:47:55.700
you're looking for
for those open roles?

00:47:55.700 --> 00:47:57.740
I would say, yes.

00:47:57.740 --> 00:48:02.360
So one, we're looking to bring
on a pricing analyst, someone

00:48:02.360 --> 00:48:04.567
to help us get smarter
on all the things

00:48:04.567 --> 00:48:06.900
that we talked about previously
with respect to pricing.

00:48:06.900 --> 00:48:09.590
And the second role is more
database marketing and sales

00:48:09.590 --> 00:48:11.360
operations focused.

00:48:11.360 --> 00:48:17.530
I think in terms
of skills, I think

00:48:17.530 --> 00:48:21.010
I need someone to be successful
in these roles in particular--

00:48:21.010 --> 00:48:24.130
and I think this does apply
to a lot of analytics focused

00:48:24.130 --> 00:48:27.520
roles in sports-- is you need
to be comfortable with wearing

00:48:27.520 --> 00:48:29.690
a couple of different hats.

00:48:29.690 --> 00:48:32.800
I am not a database
engineer by trade.

00:48:32.800 --> 00:48:36.340
But nonetheless, unlike working
at a big tech company where

00:48:36.340 --> 00:48:38.830
you've got seemingly
unlimited resources,

00:48:38.830 --> 00:48:41.260
and people can get
incredibly hyper focused

00:48:41.260 --> 00:48:44.435
and specific on very
interesting and niche problems,

00:48:44.435 --> 00:48:46.810
we need someone who is a little
bit more of a generalist,

00:48:46.810 --> 00:48:50.800
and comfortable wearing a
couple of different hats.

00:48:50.800 --> 00:48:54.010
And so people who have
background in statistics,

00:48:54.010 --> 00:48:57.730
or who have sat in the chair
at another company sport

00:48:57.730 --> 00:49:02.120
adjacent is super helpful.

00:49:02.120 --> 00:49:04.900
I think the other thing
that interesting contrast

00:49:04.900 --> 00:49:06.940
between my first
role as a consultant,

00:49:06.940 --> 00:49:10.960
and what I in my
current chair is,

00:49:10.960 --> 00:49:13.900
I get to sort of wear
the consulting hat.

00:49:13.900 --> 00:49:15.310
But we're also the operator.

00:49:15.310 --> 00:49:19.450
And so there is a
much bigger emphasis

00:49:19.450 --> 00:49:24.280
and focus on practical analytics
that we can actually deploy.

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:26.990
Whereas, at least in my
previous role as a consultant,

00:49:26.990 --> 00:49:30.400
we had to do all this
super interesting and fun

00:49:30.400 --> 00:49:31.205
analytical work.

00:49:31.205 --> 00:49:33.580
But ultimately, you kind of
hand that over to the client,

00:49:33.580 --> 00:49:36.183
and you go on to
the next project.

00:49:36.183 --> 00:49:38.350
We have the burden of
actually putting into practice

00:49:38.350 --> 00:49:41.060
what we build.

00:49:41.060 --> 00:49:43.870
And so having more of the
mindset, and sort of wanting

00:49:43.870 --> 00:49:47.650
to take ownership of the full
analytical process end to end,

00:49:47.650 --> 00:49:52.180
which is a little bit
different than when

00:49:52.180 --> 00:49:53.680
at least when I
went through school.

00:49:53.680 --> 00:49:56.592
There wasn't a ton of focus
on that last part of like how

00:49:56.592 --> 00:49:58.300
do you actually move
the business forward

00:49:58.300 --> 00:50:02.760
through the application
of analytics.

00:50:02.760 --> 00:50:04.440
Yeah.

00:50:04.440 --> 00:50:06.780
Russ, I see you just
asked a question.

00:50:06.780 --> 00:50:10.390
I'd love to turn that
over to you to ask live.

00:50:10.390 --> 00:50:10.890
Yeah.

00:50:10.890 --> 00:50:12.300
Thanks, Rachael.

00:50:12.300 --> 00:50:15.660
Chase, love the content so far.

00:50:15.660 --> 00:50:18.630
One thing that affects
a lot of us that

00:50:18.630 --> 00:50:23.070
work in financial services,
are systematic shifts in data,

00:50:23.070 --> 00:50:24.030
right?

00:50:24.030 --> 00:50:28.140
So think about something
like COVID, right?

00:50:28.140 --> 00:50:30.510
Drastically affected
consumer buying behavior.

00:50:30.510 --> 00:50:34.260
And it really wasn't something
we had seen historically

00:50:34.260 --> 00:50:36.270
in our data lakes and
data warehouses, right?

00:50:36.270 --> 00:50:40.230
So certainly complicated my
job, and my team's job a lot.

00:50:40.230 --> 00:50:44.940
Because a lot of our models were
misfiring pretty badly, right?

00:50:44.940 --> 00:50:46.577
And I think sports
analytics has always

00:50:46.577 --> 00:50:48.660
been something that's been
very interesting to me.

00:50:48.660 --> 00:50:51.450
Like full disclosure, Cleveland
Indians fan, or Guardians fan.

00:50:51.450 --> 00:50:55.140
So I still haven't
gotten over 2016 yet.

00:50:55.140 --> 00:50:58.377
You know, I've been fascinated
by ESPN'S FPI, and some

00:50:58.377 --> 00:51:00.210
of the stuff they do
in Georgia Tech related

00:51:00.210 --> 00:51:01.150
to college basketball.

00:51:01.150 --> 00:51:04.020
But I'm thinking about
specifically with the Cubs,

00:51:04.020 --> 00:51:06.330
right, and sort of like
the transitional state

00:51:06.330 --> 00:51:09.060
they're in in the
organization, specifically you

00:51:09.060 --> 00:51:13.230
know, trading away
Schwarber and Baez,

00:51:13.230 --> 00:51:15.403
and you know, I forget
the gentleman who

00:51:15.403 --> 00:51:16.320
went to San Francisco.

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:18.960
But I would have to
imagine that's going

00:51:18.960 --> 00:51:21.690
to make a lot of
your historical data

00:51:21.690 --> 00:51:24.880
a little less useful
for you and your team.

00:51:24.880 --> 00:51:26.520
Do you have any
tips or strategies?

00:51:26.520 --> 00:51:29.670
Or have you guys started
digging into this,

00:51:29.670 --> 00:51:32.970
and seeing what you can
do to make your maybe

00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:37.740
previous models better
fitting for the new reality

00:51:37.740 --> 00:51:39.620
you guys are going to be in?

00:51:39.620 --> 00:51:42.600
Yeah, that's a great question.

00:51:42.600 --> 00:51:45.540
You sort of hit on
the fundamental crux

00:51:45.540 --> 00:51:48.600
of what I think it means
to sit on the business

00:51:48.600 --> 00:51:51.180
side of a sports organization.

00:51:51.180 --> 00:51:55.170
So in an abstract
sense, all teams--

00:51:55.170 --> 00:51:57.480
sports are cyclical
by nature, right?

00:51:57.480 --> 00:51:59.110
And that's due to a
variety of reasons,

00:51:59.110 --> 00:52:02.160
mostly a lot of its team
performance related.

00:52:02.160 --> 00:52:04.650
Our job on the business
side is to smooth out

00:52:04.650 --> 00:52:06.480
those natural team
performance cycles.

00:52:06.480 --> 00:52:09.960
And when team performance
is really good,

00:52:09.960 --> 00:52:13.108
our job is to push
the business forward.

00:52:13.108 --> 00:52:15.150
And then when team
performance takes a step back,

00:52:15.150 --> 00:52:17.670
like we just saw at the
most recent trade deadline,

00:52:17.670 --> 00:52:19.740
it's to sort of
mitigate the loss,

00:52:19.740 --> 00:52:23.460
or don't let the
floor go as low.

00:52:23.460 --> 00:52:27.690
And so our job is to mitigate
sort of that natural team

00:52:27.690 --> 00:52:31.182
performance cycle and
move the business forward

00:52:31.182 --> 00:52:32.640
somewhat independent
of where we're

00:52:32.640 --> 00:52:34.800
at in any given
team performance,

00:52:34.800 --> 00:52:37.350
or competitive dynamic cycle.

00:52:37.350 --> 00:52:38.490
That's the abstract answer.

00:52:38.490 --> 00:52:40.680
And that's my guiding
light, and how

00:52:40.680 --> 00:52:44.760
I think about how I can
impact the business.

00:52:44.760 --> 00:52:48.420
More mechanically
to your point, we

00:52:48.420 --> 00:52:51.510
are in a drastically
different demand environment,

00:52:51.510 --> 00:52:54.450
and just sort of team
performance cycle

00:52:54.450 --> 00:52:58.170
today than we were
even four months ago.

00:52:58.170 --> 00:53:00.900
What I have done from a
practical standpoint is,

00:53:00.900 --> 00:53:03.180
we have pretty good
historical data.

00:53:03.180 --> 00:53:07.280
And if you go back to
2010, '11, '12, '13, '14,

00:53:07.280 --> 00:53:09.960
the Cubs were not
terribly competitive.

00:53:09.960 --> 00:53:15.390
And so what we have done is
put a little bit more emphasis

00:53:15.390 --> 00:53:23.100
on more further back in time,
but data and demand profiles

00:53:23.100 --> 00:53:27.420
that is a little bit more
representative of where

00:53:27.420 --> 00:53:29.820
we can reasonably expect
team performance to be moving

00:53:29.820 --> 00:53:32.040
forward.

00:53:32.040 --> 00:53:35.130
So just think from like a
weighting standpoint, or just

00:53:35.130 --> 00:53:37.140
the types of data
that we emphasize

00:53:37.140 --> 00:53:40.680
in training our models,
I'll say to some extent,

00:53:40.680 --> 00:53:43.650
we do try to directly
incorporate some team

00:53:43.650 --> 00:53:46.630
performance dynamic
elements into our models,

00:53:46.630 --> 00:53:50.190
whether that's expected wins,
or playoff probabilities.

00:53:50.190 --> 00:53:53.130
There's somewhat
easy things that we

00:53:53.130 --> 00:53:57.360
can calculate that we can
sort of update over time.

00:53:57.360 --> 00:53:59.880
But it's a hard problem.

00:53:59.880 --> 00:54:00.520
Yep.

00:54:00.520 --> 00:54:01.020
Definitely.

00:54:01.020 --> 00:54:01.520
Thank you.

00:54:04.160 --> 00:54:05.060
Thank you.

00:54:05.060 --> 00:54:07.400
I see Ronald asked
a great question.

00:54:07.400 --> 00:54:10.380
And I think he had to
drop to another meeting.

00:54:10.380 --> 00:54:12.140
So I'm just going to read it.

00:54:12.140 --> 00:54:15.620
But earlier when we were talking
about presenting deliverables,

00:54:15.620 --> 00:54:19.040
you mentioned Markdown,
or Markdown and Shiny.

00:54:19.040 --> 00:54:21.500
The question was, to
what extent is leadership

00:54:21.500 --> 00:54:24.350
able to process
this information?

00:54:24.350 --> 00:54:26.990
Or do you guide leadership
through the results?

00:54:26.990 --> 00:54:30.320
Along those lines, you're
providing presumably

00:54:30.320 --> 00:54:33.660
much deeper information than
the PowerPoint, Excel crowd.

00:54:33.660 --> 00:54:38.720
And how is this different
perceived by leadership?

00:54:38.720 --> 00:54:40.460
I think my best
answer to that would

00:54:40.460 --> 00:54:42.650
be, it's highly
specific based on

00:54:42.650 --> 00:54:47.030
the individual characteristics
of those individuals.

00:54:47.030 --> 00:54:50.450
And we sort of have
the full gamut of--

00:54:50.450 --> 00:54:53.720
my direct boss loves
to get into the weeds.

00:54:53.720 --> 00:54:56.540
He wants to understand
why did you choose

00:54:56.540 --> 00:54:58.190
this algorithm versus that.

00:54:58.190 --> 00:55:00.890
What happens if you
change these assumptions?

00:55:00.890 --> 00:55:04.190
And so we spend a lot of
time looking at an R-script,

00:55:04.190 --> 00:55:06.680
and like, here's
conceptually what's going on.

00:55:06.680 --> 00:55:08.180
And like, here's
the relative impact

00:55:08.180 --> 00:55:12.050
of these different assumptions.

00:55:12.050 --> 00:55:14.720
My boss sort of over indexes
on that side of things.

00:55:14.720 --> 00:55:18.823
On the other side, you've got
other individuals who don't--

00:55:18.823 --> 00:55:20.240
not they don't
have the appetite--

00:55:20.240 --> 00:55:22.282
but they're more focused
on the business outcome.

00:55:22.282 --> 00:55:24.800
And it's been somewhat
of an iterative process

00:55:24.800 --> 00:55:26.930
to understand what
resonates to what audience,

00:55:26.930 --> 00:55:32.180
and tailoring and catering
those deliverables

00:55:32.180 --> 00:55:33.530
to those individuals.

00:55:36.610 --> 00:55:38.560
Thank you.

00:55:38.560 --> 00:55:42.240
Libby, I also think you had
a question earlier around

00:55:42.240 --> 00:55:46.380
the biggest false assumptions
that people generally make.

00:55:46.380 --> 00:55:48.330
Do you want to add any
context to that one?

00:55:50.928 --> 00:55:52.720
LIBBY: I think that
I've just been thinking

00:55:52.720 --> 00:55:55.120
a lot about assumptions
lately, and how I'm usually

00:55:55.120 --> 00:55:56.860
wrong when I make
assumptions about how

00:55:56.860 --> 00:56:00.760
other organizations or
even individuals use data,

00:56:00.760 --> 00:56:05.980
and the level of complexity
that they actually use that,

00:56:05.980 --> 00:56:07.450
and the application areas.

00:56:07.450 --> 00:56:09.610
So sports is something
I'm not familiar with.

00:56:09.610 --> 00:56:12.978
And I would like to ask what
the biggest assumption is

00:56:12.978 --> 00:56:14.770
that people make that's
just totally wrong?

00:56:17.530 --> 00:56:19.570
Somewhat tongue in
cheek, but the question

00:56:19.570 --> 00:56:23.140
I still feel from friends,
family people I meet is like,

00:56:23.140 --> 00:56:25.120
am I hanging out
in the clubhouse

00:56:25.120 --> 00:56:27.940
and having dinner
with their players?

00:56:27.940 --> 00:56:33.530
And that's like could not
be farther from the truth.

00:56:33.530 --> 00:56:38.840
You know, all those guys they're
kind of doing their own thing.

00:56:38.840 --> 00:56:45.340
But I think sort more
directly to your point

00:56:45.340 --> 00:56:49.300
on assumptions about our
core business operations,

00:56:49.300 --> 00:56:57.480
I think people have a lot
of assumptions about where

00:56:57.480 --> 00:57:02.070
decisions are made, and
sort of who is running

00:57:02.070 --> 00:57:03.540
the show to some extent.

00:57:03.540 --> 00:57:07.440
And pretty much all of
your major sports teams

00:57:07.440 --> 00:57:10.110
are in some capacity owned
by private ownership group.

00:57:10.110 --> 00:57:13.500
And those owners have different
media personalities themselves,

00:57:13.500 --> 00:57:16.530
and individual,
and family brands.

00:57:16.530 --> 00:57:18.275
That depending on
the organization,

00:57:18.275 --> 00:57:19.650
they may or may
not have anything

00:57:19.650 --> 00:57:22.560
to do with the
day-to-day operations.

00:57:22.560 --> 00:57:28.140
But nonetheless, if you follow
Twitter, sort of major media

00:57:28.140 --> 00:57:32.520
outlets, the characterization
of the decision making process

00:57:32.520 --> 00:57:34.890
as it gets described
by the media

00:57:34.890 --> 00:57:41.250
is often pretty far from what I
observe on a day-to-day basis.

00:57:41.250 --> 00:57:43.950
And by and large, it's
one of those things

00:57:43.950 --> 00:57:47.190
that we're not really
equipped, or would necessarily

00:57:47.190 --> 00:57:50.510
be in our best interest to try
and change those perceptions.

00:57:50.510 --> 00:57:55.000
So we just kind of shrug
your shoulders and go on.

00:57:55.000 --> 00:57:57.720
But it's interesting
seeing how stuff

00:57:57.720 --> 00:58:01.500
that I am directly involved
in behind the scenes

00:58:01.500 --> 00:58:03.483
ends up getting
portrayed in the media.

00:58:03.483 --> 00:58:04.650
And sometimes, it's spot on.

00:58:04.650 --> 00:58:06.837
And other times, you're
just kind of like,

00:58:06.837 --> 00:58:07.920
that's not how this works.

00:58:13.710 --> 00:58:14.670
Thank you, Chase.

00:58:14.670 --> 00:58:16.500
As we get to the
top of the hour,

00:58:16.500 --> 00:58:18.667
I know some people
might have to run.

00:58:18.667 --> 00:58:20.250
So I just want to
ask, what's the best

00:58:20.250 --> 00:58:22.710
way to get in touch with
you if people have follow up

00:58:22.710 --> 00:58:26.070
questions, or if they're
interested in any of the roles?

00:58:26.070 --> 00:58:27.690
Is it LinkedIn?

00:58:27.690 --> 00:58:30.850
Yeah, I'm pretty easy
to track down on LinkedIn.

00:58:30.850 --> 00:58:34.200
So I please encourage anyone to
reach out via LinkedIn if you

00:58:34.200 --> 00:58:38.412
have any questions specific to
the role certainly, but more

00:58:38.412 --> 00:58:39.870
generally, if you
have an interest,

00:58:39.870 --> 00:58:43.620
or just want to learn more
about the sports industry.

00:58:43.620 --> 00:58:45.360
I obviously know the
most about baseball,

00:58:45.360 --> 00:58:49.980
but have some decent perspective
on other leagues as well.

00:58:49.980 --> 00:58:51.480
But yeah, please do reach out.

00:58:51.480 --> 00:58:57.350
And I'm usually pretty
good about responding.

00:58:57.350 --> 00:58:57.950
Awesome.

00:58:57.950 --> 00:58:59.150
Thank you, Chase.

00:58:59.150 --> 00:59:02.180
If it's OK, can I ask you
maybe one or two more questions

00:59:02.180 --> 00:59:02.810
as we--

00:59:02.810 --> 00:59:06.830
I just want to make
sure I didn't miss any.

00:59:06.830 --> 00:59:11.690
I'd love to hear what advice
you have for maybe someone who's

00:59:11.690 --> 00:59:14.510
on the call right now that's
hoping to get into a leadership

00:59:14.510 --> 00:59:17.690
position, and how
they could move

00:59:17.690 --> 00:59:20.690
from being that individual
contributor into leadership?

00:59:23.380 --> 00:59:28.480
I feel like the biggest
jump there, it's probably not

00:59:28.480 --> 00:59:32.050
in the hard skills category.

00:59:32.050 --> 00:59:33.760
I think in order to
move up, I sort of

00:59:33.760 --> 00:59:36.010
think of the hard
skill, you know,

00:59:36.010 --> 00:59:38.933
the proficiency and
excellence in what I think

00:59:38.933 --> 00:59:41.350
is like the hard data science
type skills, that's somewhat

00:59:41.350 --> 00:59:45.490
of a table stakes criteria.

00:59:45.490 --> 00:59:49.690
So you need to show a sort of
minimum level of competency,

00:59:49.690 --> 00:59:52.420
or excellence in that category.

00:59:52.420 --> 00:59:54.460
But where I think you can
start to differentiate

00:59:54.460 --> 00:59:58.090
yourself is more
in the soft skills.

00:59:58.090 --> 00:59:59.300
It's the leadership.

00:59:59.300 --> 01:00:00.700
It's the negotiation.

01:00:00.700 --> 01:00:03.410
It's the team building.

01:00:03.410 --> 01:00:09.850
It's sort of all the stuff
that can be described

01:00:09.850 --> 01:00:12.340
as like the soft fluffy skills.

01:00:12.340 --> 01:00:14.410
But when you think about--

01:00:14.410 --> 01:00:17.290
I think high-performing
organizations have tightly

01:00:17.290 --> 01:00:21.200
aligned strategy and
data analytics functions.

01:00:21.200 --> 01:00:24.820
And so when I think about
what does aspiring leader need

01:00:24.820 --> 01:00:27.460
to do, it's starting
to develop a knack

01:00:27.460 --> 01:00:30.400
for what is the strategy
of the organization,

01:00:30.400 --> 01:00:32.230
and how can I influence that?

01:00:32.230 --> 01:00:35.500
Data, in a lot of ways is a
tool that can very meaningfully

01:00:35.500 --> 01:00:39.358
improve organizations,
but I think

01:00:39.358 --> 01:00:40.900
you can really
differentiate yourself

01:00:40.900 --> 01:00:46.810
on outperforming your
competitors in some sense,

01:00:46.810 --> 01:00:47.830
in those soft skills.

01:00:51.408 --> 01:00:52.200
Thank you, Chase.

01:00:52.200 --> 01:00:55.650
And I just want to say, if I've
missed anybody's questions,

01:00:55.650 --> 01:00:59.220
I'm trying to [INAUDIBLE]
in the chat here.

01:00:59.220 --> 01:01:01.410
But please feel free to
just unmute yourself,

01:01:01.410 --> 01:01:02.863
and jump in live too.

01:01:07.030 --> 01:01:09.200
I'll do a quick check.

01:01:09.200 --> 01:01:11.030
I don't see anyone that unmuted.

01:01:11.030 --> 01:01:13.820
One last question, Chase,
I'd love to ask you.

01:01:13.820 --> 01:01:16.670
I'm trying to help bring
more resources together

01:01:16.670 --> 01:01:19.940
for someone who's just
trying to start advocating

01:01:19.940 --> 01:01:21.950
for data science internally.

01:01:21.950 --> 01:01:26.210
And maybe they're the
first data person.

01:01:26.210 --> 01:01:31.790
How would you I guess, tell
someone to go about that

01:01:31.790 --> 01:01:34.700
process if they're in a position
where they actually just

01:01:34.700 --> 01:01:37.340
have to start at getting
approval to even use

01:01:37.340 --> 01:01:39.590
some of these tools?

01:01:39.590 --> 01:01:44.160
Yeah, I think where
my head initially goes--

01:01:44.160 --> 01:01:47.790
and this goes back to
some of my MBA classes--

01:01:47.790 --> 01:01:49.950
but I think it's a
pretty simple framework

01:01:49.950 --> 01:01:55.890
of what's the ROI that you think
you could reasonably drive.

01:01:55.890 --> 01:01:57.720
So I would think
about it as engaging

01:01:57.720 --> 01:02:01.250
with sort of the
major stakeholders

01:02:01.250 --> 01:02:02.840
in an organization,
and understanding

01:02:02.840 --> 01:02:04.760
what are your
problems, and basically

01:02:04.760 --> 01:02:11.060
how you think about it in terms
of revenue, or just efficiency,

01:02:11.060 --> 01:02:11.590
right?

01:02:11.590 --> 01:02:14.575
If this business unit is
operating at 80% efficiency,

01:02:14.575 --> 01:02:15.950
what does it mean
to the business

01:02:15.950 --> 01:02:20.310
if we can move
that to 85% or 90%?

01:02:20.310 --> 01:02:23.850
Think about some way
to quantify that.

01:02:23.850 --> 01:02:28.380
Balance that up
against what resources

01:02:28.380 --> 01:02:32.370
you need to bring to the
table to unlock that 5% or 10%

01:02:32.370 --> 01:02:33.570
improvement.

01:02:33.570 --> 01:02:35.100
I think about that
in the broadest

01:02:35.100 --> 01:02:36.690
sense of its headcount.

01:02:36.690 --> 01:02:37.500
Its technology.

01:02:37.500 --> 01:02:39.300
Its investment and
data warehousing.

01:02:39.300 --> 01:02:42.550
Sort of depending on where that
organization is, that could

01:02:42.550 --> 01:02:43.800
go in a lot of different ways.

01:02:43.800 --> 01:02:53.660
But for my group, we think
about driving essentially,

01:02:53.660 --> 01:02:56.780
a 5x return on the investments
that the organization make

01:02:56.780 --> 01:02:57.470
in our group.

01:02:57.470 --> 01:02:59.750
And that's sort of the
benchmark and the threshold

01:02:59.750 --> 01:03:02.990
that I'm held accountable to.

01:03:02.990 --> 01:03:06.380
It's worked well here.

01:03:06.380 --> 01:03:09.260
And I think it's a good
sort of forcing function

01:03:09.260 --> 01:03:13.160
to make sure you're focusing
your time on the right problems

01:03:13.160 --> 01:03:17.570
that can sort of meaningfully
drive the business forward.

01:03:17.570 --> 01:03:18.590
Definitely.

01:03:18.590 --> 01:03:19.760
Thank you so much, Chase.

01:03:19.760 --> 01:03:23.120
I really appreciate you
joining and jumping on here,

01:03:23.120 --> 01:03:25.700
and sharing all your
insights with us.

01:03:25.700 --> 01:03:29.090
I hope to get everybody together
in person at some point too.

01:03:29.090 --> 01:03:30.920
And I know we talked
about maybe next year

01:03:30.920 --> 01:03:33.890
there's the Sloan
Conference, Sports Analytics

01:03:33.890 --> 01:03:35.390
conference in Boston,
maybe planning

01:03:35.390 --> 01:03:37.310
a meetup or something
around that,

01:03:37.310 --> 01:03:40.670
as well if other people are
going to be in the area.

01:03:40.670 --> 01:03:41.750
But thank you so much.

01:03:41.750 --> 01:03:43.160
Really appreciate it.

01:03:43.160 --> 01:03:43.688
Yeah.

01:03:43.688 --> 01:03:44.480
Thank you, Rachael.

01:03:44.480 --> 01:03:46.140
And thanks, everyone.

01:03:46.140 --> 01:03:48.200
Great questions
and dialogue today.

01:03:48.200 --> 01:03:49.310
I really enjoyed it.

01:03:49.310 --> 01:03:50.990
So thank you all.

01:03:50.990 --> 01:03:53.810
Have a great rest
of the day, everyone.

